{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>Behold, to bless I have received, and I bless, etc.</b> According to what we explained in the verse (Numbers 23:5) ״וַיָּשֶׂם ה׳ דָּבָר בְּפִי בִלְעָם״ — that He placed in his mouth a spiritual דָּבָר that would interpose between his mouth and the holy spirit that speaks through him — this is what the Sages, may their memory be blessed, intended in their saying (Yalkut Shim'oni): דָּבָר — an angel. Except that they gave the reason for the matter in order to prevent him from cursing, and both explanations are correct. Accordingly, the statement <b>Behold, to bless I have received</b> intends that he felt what was added in his mouth and on his tongue — that he would utter blessing. Further, ״וּבֵרֵךְ״ — for the דָּבָר placed in his mouth did not leave the matter to Balaam's own will, but rather ״וּבֵרֵךְ״ — meaning, it compels him to bless; and even though he has no desire for it, he is unable to reverse it. And this is the meaning of His saying <b>and I will not reverse it</b> — meaning, he cannot turn it back."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:13:20.949376156Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}